<!--META-DATA
{
	"tags": ["monte-carlo", "probability", "birthday-problem", "simulation", "function"]
}
-->

<statement>
	<p>
		This function estimates the probability that in a group of <m>k</m> people, at least two share the same birthday — a classic probability puzzle known as the <q>birthday problem</q>.
	</p>

	<tabular valign="top" halign="right" top="minor">
		<col width="12%"/>
		<col width="88%" halign="left"/>

		<row>
			<cell><p>Inputs:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p><pf>k</pf> (1 x 1) integer — number of people in the room.</p>
				<p><pf>trials</pf> (1 x 1) integer — number of Monte Carlo simulations to run.</p>
				<p><pf>seed</pf> (optional, 1 x 1 integer) — random number generator seed for reproducibility.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>

		<row bottom="minor">
			<cell><p>Outputs:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p><pf>prob</pf> (1 x 1) double — estimated probability that at least two of the <pf>k</pf> people share a birthday.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell><p>Details:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p>▸ Each trial simulates a group of <pf>k</pf> people, where each person’s birthday is modeled as a random integer between 1 and 365 (assuming no leap years).</p>
			</cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell/>
			<cell><p>▸ A Boolean array <pf>seen</pf> keeps track of which birthdays have been observed so far.</p></cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell/>
			<cell><p>▸ If a generated birthday has already been seen, a duplicate is detected and the trial is counted as a "hit."</p></cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell/>
			<cell><p>▸ The loop breaks early once a duplicate is found to improve efficiency.</p></cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell/>
			<cell><p>▸ The estimated probability is calculated as <m>\text{hits} / \text{trials}</m>.</p></cell>
		</row>

		<row bottom="minor">
			<cell><p>Notes:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p>▸ If <pf>seed</pf> is provided, the random number generator is initialized with <pf>rng(seed,'twister')</pf> for reproducible results.</p>
				<p>▸ The true theoretical probability is <m>1 - \frac{365!}{(365-k)! \, 365^k}</m>, but this simulation approximates it through repeated random sampling.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>
	</tabular>

	<p>
		Examples:
		<program>
			<code>
				% Example 1: Estimate the probability that two people share a birthday in a group of 23
				prob = prob_same_bday(23, 10000)
				% returns a value close to 0.507
			</code>
		</program>

		<program>
			<code>
				% Example 2: Reproducible simulation with fixed seed
				prob = prob_same_bday(30, 1e6, 123)
				% returns approximately 0.706
			</code>
		</program>
	</p>
</statement>
